1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,025
Imagine you go for a routine health check,

2
00:00:03,025 --> 00:00:05,450
and the doctor suggests a quick screening test.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,167
It looks for a condition that is fairly rare.

4
00:00:08,167 --> 00:00:11,250
About one in every hundred people your age has it.

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:14,193
The test is good. If you have the condition,

6
00:00:14,193 --> 00:00:16,370
it catches it nine times out of ten.

7
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:20,426
And if you are healthy, it only gives a false alarm one time in

8
00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:20,820
ten.

9
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:23,048
A few days later, the result comes back.

10
00:00:23,048 --> 00:00:23,660
Positive.

11
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:26,294
So, how worried should you be?

12
00:00:26,294 --> 00:00:28,220
What is the chance you actually have it?

13
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:30,770
Take a second, and make your own guess.

14
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:34,223
Most people say about ninety percent.

15
00:00:34,223 --> 00:00:37,090
After all, the test is ninety percent accurate.

16
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:41,140
Here's the surprise. The real answer is about eight percent.

17
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:46,330
Not ninety. Eight. Less than one in ten.

18
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:49,612
Nothing in that story is a trick.

19
00:00:49,612 --> 00:00:51,207
The test really is good.

20
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:53,490
Your gut just left out one number.

21
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,920
How rare the condition was to begin with.

22
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,958
The tool that puts that number back in is called Bayes' theorem.

23
00:00:59,958 --> 00:01:03,184
And by the end of this video, you will be able to do this in

24
00:01:03,184 --> 00:01:03,780
your head.

25
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,308
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

26
00:01:06,308 --> 00:01:08,490
Where math is something you can see.

27
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:12,236
Today you will learn how to turn a test result into a real

28
00:01:12,236 --> 00:01:14,580
probability, just by counting people.

29
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:17,415
You will see Bayes' theorem as a formula,

30
00:01:17,415 --> 00:01:19,900
and exactly what every symbol means.

31
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:24,018
And you will see how evidence adds up, in spam filters,

32
00:01:24,018 --> 00:01:26,140
fraud alerts and machine learning.

33
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:28,330
This isn't just about medical tests.

34
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:31,833
Any time a rare thing meets an imperfect detector,

35
00:01:31,833 --> 00:01:33,310
the same trap is waiting.

36
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:35,363
A smoke alarm going off.

37
00:01:35,363 --> 00:01:37,312
An email flagged as spam.

38
00:01:37,312 --> 00:01:40,470
A message from your bank asking, was this you?

39
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:43,217
Each one is a positive test.

40
00:01:43,217 --> 00:01:47,045
And each one asks the same question: given this alarm,

41
00:01:47,045 --> 00:01:49,200
how likely is the real thing?

42
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:51,776
And smart people get this wrong.

43
00:01:51,776 --> 00:01:55,610
In one well known study, doctors were given a screening puzzle

44
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:56,960
with numbers like ours.

45
00:01:57,310 --> 00:01:59,719
Only about one in five picked the right answer.

46
00:01:59,719 --> 00:02:01,190
Almost half picked nine in ten.

47
00:02:02,190 --> 00:02:04,677
That's not because doctors are bad at math.

48
00:02:04,677 --> 00:02:08,400
It's because the numbers came as percentages of percentages.

49
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:11,902
Change the form, and the problem becomes easy.

50
00:02:11,902 --> 00:02:15,286
So here is the trick for this whole video: stop thinking in

51
00:02:15,286 --> 00:02:17,760
percentages, and start counting people.

52
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:20,827
And Bayes isn't only about tests.

53
00:02:20,827 --> 00:02:24,289
It's a rule for changing your mind by the right amount when new

54
00:02:24,289 --> 00:02:27,950
evidence arrives. Not too little, and not too much.

55
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:31,509
Let's do it. Picture one thousand people,

56
00:02:31,509 --> 00:02:33,000
all taking the same test.

57
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,410
Each little square is one person.

58
00:02:35,410 --> 00:02:37,690
Forty across, twenty five down.

59
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:38,830
One thousand.

60
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:42,120
Step one. How many of them have the condition?

61
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:44,510
One in a hundred. So, ten people.

62
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:47,200
The other nine hundred and ninety are healthy.

63
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:50,790
Step two. Test the ten people who have the condition.

64
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:52,410
It catches nine of them.

65
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:56,470
So, nine true positives, and one person the test misses.

66
00:02:56,820 --> 00:03:00,527
Step three. Test the nine hundred and ninety healthy people.

67
00:03:00,527 --> 00:03:03,080
It wrongly flags one in ten of them.

68
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:05,120
That's ninety nine false alarms.

69
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:09,910
Now, look only at the people holding a positive result.

70
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:12,267
Nine of them actually have the condition.

71
00:03:12,267 --> 00:03:13,787
Ninety nine are healthy.

72
00:03:13,787 --> 00:03:16,120
One hundred and eight positives in total.

73
00:03:16,470 --> 00:03:19,887
If your test is positive, you are one of these one hundred and

74
00:03:19,887 --> 00:03:22,010
eight. And only nine of them are sick.

75
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:24,001
Nine out of one hundred and eight.

76
00:03:24,001 --> 00:03:26,020
That's one in twelve, about eight percent.

77
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:31,560
The test did its job. It caught almost everyone who was sick.

78
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:36,358
But healthy people outnumber sick people ninety nine to one.

79
00:03:36,358 --> 00:03:40,322
So even a small false alarm rate creates a crowd of false

80
00:03:40,322 --> 00:03:41,070
alarms.

81
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:45,610
Here, the false alarms outnumber the real cases eleven to one.

82
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:50,721
And yes, the test really is ninety percent accurate overall.

83
00:03:50,721 --> 00:03:54,613
Nine true positives, plus eight hundred and ninety one healthy

84
00:03:54,613 --> 00:03:56,056
people correctly cleared.

85
00:03:56,056 --> 00:03:58,520
Nine hundred right answers out of a thousand.

86
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:02,517
And notice, the positive result still mattered.

87
00:04:02,517 --> 00:04:05,513
Before the test, your chance was one in a hundred.

88
00:04:05,513 --> 00:04:07,330
After it, one in twelve.

89
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:09,525
That's more than eight times higher.

90
00:04:09,525 --> 00:04:11,166
The test moved you a lot.

91
00:04:11,166 --> 00:04:13,620
It just didn't move you anywhere near certain.

92
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:17,565
And a negative result? Of the eight hundred and ninety two

93
00:04:17,565 --> 00:04:20,250
people who test negative, just one has the condition.

94
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:23,649
So a negative test drops your chance from one in a hundred to

95
00:04:23,649 --> 00:04:25,304
about one in nine hundred.

96
00:04:25,304 --> 00:04:26,600
That's very reassuring.

97
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:31,962
What we just did, counting people, is exactly Bayes' theorem.

98
00:04:31,962 --> 00:04:34,470
Let's write it the way you'll see it in books.

99
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:36,507
Here is the same crowd as a tree.

100
00:04:36,507 --> 00:04:38,927
One thousand people split into ten who have it,

101
00:04:38,927 --> 00:04:40,980
and nine hundred and ninety who don't.

102
00:04:41,330 --> 00:04:43,467
Then each branch splits again by the test result.

103
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:46,823
Nine and one on top. Ninety nine and eight hundred and ninety

104
00:04:46,823 --> 00:04:47,740
one below.

105
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:51,501
A positive result means you're on one of the two positive

106
00:04:51,501 --> 00:04:53,767
leaves. Nine, or ninety nine.

107
00:04:53,767 --> 00:04:54,990
That's all Bayes is doing.

108
00:04:55,990 --> 00:04:59,108
First, the symbols. A capital P, with brackets,

109
00:04:59,108 --> 00:05:01,940
means the probability of whatever is inside.

110
00:05:02,290 --> 00:05:04,371
A means having the condition.

111
00:05:04,371 --> 00:05:06,220
B means testing positive.

112
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:08,630
And this vertical bar means given.

113
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:12,340
So, P of A given B is the chance you have the condition,

114
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:14,400
given that you tested positive.

115
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:16,365
That's the number we want.

116
00:05:16,365 --> 00:05:17,980
And here is Bayes' theorem.

117
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:24,587
P of A given B equals P of B given A, times P of A,

118
00:05:24,587 --> 00:05:26,710
divided by P of B.

119
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:29,150
Now let's match each piece to our crowd.

120
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:31,162
P of A is the base rate.

121
00:05:31,162 --> 00:05:34,060
How common the condition is before any test.

122
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:35,310
Ten in a thousand.

123
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:39,352
P of B given A is how good the test is at catching it.

124
00:05:39,352 --> 00:05:40,440
Nine out of ten.

125
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:43,942
Multiply those two, and you get the true positives.

126
00:05:43,942 --> 00:05:45,130
Nine in a thousand.

127
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:49,808
P of B, on the bottom, is the chance of testing positive at all.

128
00:05:49,808 --> 00:05:53,100
Real or false, that's one hundred and eight in a thousand.

129
00:05:53,450 --> 00:05:55,761
Divide, and the thousands cancel.

130
00:05:55,761 --> 00:05:58,085
Nine out of one hundred and eight.

131
00:05:58,085 --> 00:06:00,410
The same answer we got by counting.

132
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:05,658
So the formula is just a short way of saying: real positives,

133
00:06:05,658 --> 00:06:07,630
divided by all positives.

134
00:06:08,630 --> 00:06:10,397
The bottom part is where people slip.

135
00:06:10,397 --> 00:06:11,970
It hides the false alarms.

136
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:16,673
Written out in full, P of B is the true positives from the sick,

137
00:06:16,673 --> 00:06:18,850
plus the false alarms from the healthy.

138
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,506
Three names you'll hear.

139
00:06:20,506 --> 00:06:22,462
The base rate is called the prior.

140
00:06:22,462 --> 00:06:24,853
The answer is called the posterior.

141
00:06:24,853 --> 00:06:28,330
And P of B given A is called the likelihood.

142
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:30,860
Prior means before the evidence.

143
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:32,550
Posterior means after.

144
00:06:33,550 --> 00:06:37,441
So you tested positive, and your chance is now about one in

145
00:06:37,441 --> 00:06:40,040
twelve. What would a sensible doctor do next?

146
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:43,212
Test again. And here is the beautiful part.

147
00:06:43,212 --> 00:06:46,460
The answer from the first test becomes the starting point for

148
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:47,330
the second.

149
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,040
Yesterday's posterior is today's prior.

150
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:53,281
Take our one hundred and eight positives.

151
00:06:53,281 --> 00:06:55,741
Nine are sick, ninety nine are healthy.

152
00:06:55,741 --> 00:06:57,140
They all take a second test.

153
00:06:57,490 --> 00:07:00,836
To avoid fractions of people, picture ten times as many.

154
00:07:00,836 --> 00:07:04,156
Ninety people who have it, and nine hundred and ninety who

155
00:07:04,156 --> 00:07:04,570
don't.

156
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:06,882
The test catches nine in ten of the sick,

157
00:07:06,882 --> 00:07:09,193
and wrongly flags one in ten of the healthy.

158
00:07:09,193 --> 00:07:10,110
Same as before.

159
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:12,857
After a second positive: eighty one sick,

160
00:07:12,857 --> 00:07:14,130
and ninety nine healthy.

161
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:16,889
Eighty one out of one hundred and eighty.

162
00:07:16,889 --> 00:07:18,120
That's forty five percent.

163
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:22,925
One percent, then eight, then forty five.

164
00:07:22,925 --> 00:07:25,790
Each positive pushes the number up.

165
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:27,769
There's a neat shortcut here.

166
00:07:27,769 --> 00:07:29,600
Compare sick to healthy as odds.

167
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:34,361
Before any test, the odds are one sick person for every ninety

168
00:07:34,361 --> 00:07:35,180
nine healthy.

169
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:39,232
A sick person is nine times more likely to test positive than a

170
00:07:39,232 --> 00:07:42,230
healthy one. Ninety percent, versus ten.

171
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:45,676
So each positive multiplies the odds of having it by nine.

172
00:07:45,676 --> 00:07:48,772
One to ninety nine becomes nine to ninety nine,

173
00:07:48,772 --> 00:07:50,790
then eighty one to ninety nine.

174
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:54,060
A third positive makes it seven hundred and twenty nine to

175
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:56,390
ninety nine. About eighty eight percent.

176
00:07:57,390 --> 00:07:59,153
One important condition.

177
00:07:59,153 --> 00:08:02,047
This only works if the second test makes its mistakes

178
00:08:02,047 --> 00:08:03,810
independently of the first.

179
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:07,100
If you repeat the same test on the same sample,

180
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:10,040
it might simply repeat the same false alarm.

181
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:12,980
Then the second positive tells you much less.

182
00:08:13,330 --> 00:08:16,778
That's one reason doctors often confirm with a different kind of

183
00:08:16,778 --> 00:08:17,190
test.

184
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:20,444
So what actually moves the answer?

185
00:08:20,444 --> 00:08:22,089
There are only three dials.

186
00:08:22,089 --> 00:08:25,190
The base rate, the hit rate, and the false alarm rate.

187
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:29,207
Let's turn the base rate dial first, and keep the test exactly

188
00:08:29,207 --> 00:08:29,920
the same.

189
00:08:30,270 --> 00:08:32,890
At one in a hundred, we're at eight percent.

190
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:35,510
At one in ten, it jumps to fifty percent.

191
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:37,692
Let's check that middle one by counting.

192
00:08:37,692 --> 00:08:40,819
With one in ten, a thousand people means one hundred sick,

193
00:08:40,819 --> 00:08:42,830
and the test catches ninety of them.

194
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:46,264
Nine hundred healthy, and one in ten flagged.

195
00:08:46,264 --> 00:08:50,009
Ninety false alarms. Ninety real, ninety false.

196
00:08:50,009 --> 00:08:51,110
Fifty percent.

197
00:08:52,110 --> 00:08:54,718
And only when half the people have the condition does your gut's

198
00:08:54,718 --> 00:08:56,850
ninety percent finally become right.

199
00:08:58,350 --> 00:09:01,750
The rarer the condition, the more the false alarms win.

200
00:09:02,750 --> 00:09:04,899
Now turn the false alarm dial instead.

201
00:09:04,899 --> 00:09:08,768
Same base rate, one in a hundred, but a test that wrongly flags

202
00:09:08,768 --> 00:09:11,560
only one healthy person in a hundred.

203
00:09:11,910 --> 00:09:15,196
Now there are about ten false alarms instead of ninety nine,

204
00:09:15,196 --> 00:09:17,760
and a positive means about forty eight percent.

205
00:09:18,110 --> 00:09:21,447
And the hit rate dial? Push it from nine in ten all the way to

206
00:09:21,447 --> 00:09:24,172
perfect, and the answer barely moves.

207
00:09:24,172 --> 00:09:26,470
From about eight percent to about nine.

208
00:09:27,470 --> 00:09:30,951
So, a rule of thumb. When the thing you're looking for is rare,

209
00:09:30,951 --> 00:09:33,790
the false alarm rate matters far more than the hit rate.

210
00:09:35,290 --> 00:09:38,345
Now let's see where this shows up in the real world.

211
00:09:38,345 --> 00:09:41,620
The labels change, but the counting is the same.

212
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:46,250
Machine learning. A classifier is just a test run by a computer.

213
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:48,946
Engineers have names for our numbers.

214
00:09:48,946 --> 00:09:51,930
Catching nine out of ten sick people is called recall.

215
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:55,367
And nine out of one hundred and eight, the share of alarms that

216
00:09:55,367 --> 00:09:57,420
are real, is called precision.

217
00:09:57,770 --> 00:09:59,907
When the thing you're looking for is rare,

218
00:09:59,907 --> 00:10:03,322
a model can have great recall and still terrible precision.

219
00:10:03,322 --> 00:10:04,820
That's the base rate at work.

220
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:09,137
That's why teams measure precision on data with the real base

221
00:10:09,137 --> 00:10:13,556
rate. On a balanced test set, half positive and half negative,

222
00:10:13,556 --> 00:10:16,650
our same test would show ninety percent precision.

223
00:10:16,650 --> 00:10:19,080
In the real world, about eight percent.

224
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:23,590
And here's a trap. A model that simply says healthy to everyone

225
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:27,320
is ninety nine percent accurate, and completely useless.

226
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:32,150
Data science. Bayes is the engine of Bayesian statistics.

227
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:36,156
You start with a prior belief, say, about how often visitors to

228
00:10:36,156 --> 00:10:39,781
a website buy something, and update it as each day's data comes

229
00:10:39,781 --> 00:10:40,190
in.

230
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:43,628
Each day's posterior becomes the next day's prior.

231
00:10:43,628 --> 00:10:45,760
Exactly like our second test.

232
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:49,999
And before trusting any automated flag on a dashboard,

233
00:10:49,999 --> 00:10:52,514
a good analyst asks: of all the past flags,

234
00:10:52,514 --> 00:10:55,029
how many were real? That's precision again,

235
00:10:55,029 --> 00:10:56,570
measured on real data.

236
00:10:56,920 --> 00:11:00,230
Finance. Banks run fraud detectors on card payments.

237
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:04,020
Fraud is rare, far rarer than one in a hundred payments.

238
00:11:04,020 --> 00:11:07,620
So even a detector as good as our test would flag mostly honest

239
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:08,240
payments.

240
00:11:08,590 --> 00:11:11,411
That's one reason many banks send a quick,

241
00:11:11,411 --> 00:11:15,162
was this you, message instead of simply blocking the card.

242
00:11:15,162 --> 00:11:19,370
In Bayes terms, your reply is a second, independent test.

243
00:11:20,370 --> 00:11:23,051
The same idea applies to market signals.

244
00:11:23,051 --> 00:11:26,853
A warning sign that fired before most crashes may also fire in

245
00:11:26,853 --> 00:11:30,653
many calm years. Always ask how often it fires when nothing

246
00:11:30,653 --> 00:11:31,320
happens.

247
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:36,441
Computer science. Some of the first really effective spam

248
00:11:36,441 --> 00:11:38,890
filters were built on Bayes' theorem.

249
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:41,698
The prior is how much of all email is spam.

250
00:11:41,698 --> 00:11:43,750
The evidence is the words inside.

251
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:47,626
Each word nudges the odds up or down, just like each positive

252
00:11:47,626 --> 00:11:50,490
test. The filter multiplies them together.

253
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:55,765
To keep it simple, it treats every word as independent of the

254
00:11:55,765 --> 00:11:59,526
others. That shortcut is why it's called naive Bayes.

255
00:11:59,526 --> 00:12:03,010
It isn't true, but it works surprisingly well.

256
00:12:04,010 --> 00:12:06,334
Computer security has the same problem.

257
00:12:06,334 --> 00:12:09,665
An intrusion alarm that fires on just one in a thousand normal

258
00:12:09,665 --> 00:12:12,690
logins can still bury the few real attacks under false alarms.

259
00:12:13,690 --> 00:12:17,022
Before we wrap up, here are the three most common mistakes

260
00:12:17,022 --> 00:12:18,020
people make with Bayes.

261
00:12:18,370 --> 00:12:20,650
Mistake one: flipping the question.

262
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,540
The chance of a positive test, if you're sick,

263
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:25,889
is ninety percent. The chance you're sick,

264
00:12:25,889 --> 00:12:28,620
if you test positive, is about eight percent.

265
00:12:28,970 --> 00:12:30,510
Same words, swapped around.

266
00:12:30,510 --> 00:12:32,380
Completely different numbers.

267
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:36,935
In courtrooms, this flip has a name: the prosecutor's fallacy.

268
00:12:36,935 --> 00:12:40,010
The chance of the evidence if someone is innocent is not the

269
00:12:40,010 --> 00:12:42,440
chance they're innocent, given the evidence.

270
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:45,960
Mistake two: ignoring the base rate.

271
00:12:46,310 --> 00:12:49,250
Take the same test, but give it only to patients who already

272
00:12:49,250 --> 00:12:52,240
have symptoms. Say one in five of them has the condition.

273
00:12:52,590 --> 00:12:56,516
Out of a thousand, that's one hundred and eighty true positives,

274
00:12:56,516 --> 00:12:58,092
against eighty false alarms.

275
00:12:58,092 --> 00:13:00,830
Now a positive means about seventy percent.

276
00:13:01,180 --> 00:13:02,791
Same test. Same result.

277
00:13:02,791 --> 00:13:05,120
The base rate changed everything.

278
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:10,180
Mistake three: counting the same clue twice.

279
00:13:10,530 --> 00:13:14,008
If two tests share the same weakness, or two news stories copy

280
00:13:14,008 --> 00:13:17,706
the same source, they are really one piece of evidence,

281
00:13:17,706 --> 00:13:18,360
not two.

282
00:13:18,710 --> 00:13:20,924
Multiply them as if they were independent,

283
00:13:20,924 --> 00:13:22,640
and you'll end up far too confident.

284
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:27,218
And a bonus mistake: deciding the test is useless because the

285
00:13:27,218 --> 00:13:28,930
answer was only eight percent.

286
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:32,554
It isn't. A positive raised the chance more than eightfold,

287
00:13:32,554 --> 00:13:35,608
and a negative almost rules the condition out.

288
00:13:35,608 --> 00:13:37,245
A good test is a strong clue.

289
00:13:37,245 --> 00:13:38,680
It's just not a verdict.

290
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:42,858
Let's turn all of that into a recipe you can use for any yes or

291
00:13:42,858 --> 00:13:43,580
no question.

292
00:13:43,930 --> 00:13:46,225
Step one: start with the base rate.

293
00:13:46,225 --> 00:13:49,680
How common is the thing, before any evidence?

294
00:13:50,030 --> 00:13:52,097
Step two: picture a crowd.

295
00:13:52,097 --> 00:13:55,570
One thousand people usually avoids fractions.

296
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:58,416
Step three: split the crowd with the test.

297
00:13:58,416 --> 00:14:01,940
Count the true positives, and the false alarms.

298
00:14:02,290 --> 00:14:05,755
Step four: keep only the people who match the evidence,

299
00:14:05,755 --> 00:14:09,440
and divide. Real cases, over all positives.

300
00:14:09,790 --> 00:14:13,981
Step five: update. When new, independent evidence arrives,

301
00:14:13,981 --> 00:14:16,270
your answer becomes the new base rate.

302
00:14:17,770 --> 00:14:20,603
For our test: one in a hundred to start.

303
00:14:20,603 --> 00:14:23,872
Nine real positives, ninety nine false alarms.

304
00:14:23,872 --> 00:14:25,180
About eight percent.

305
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:29,227
A test result is a clue, not a verdict.

306
00:14:29,227 --> 00:14:31,580
Bayes tells you how much the clue is worth.

307
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:37,451
Look around your world, and notice how many alarms are false

308
00:14:37,451 --> 00:14:40,630
alarms, and how rare the real thing usually is.

309
00:14:41,230 --> 00:14:43,293
If this lesson made things clear for you,

310
00:14:43,293 --> 00:14:46,176
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

311
00:14:46,176 --> 00:14:47,010
together.

312
00:14:47,810 --> 00:14:49,270
But here's a puzzle for next time.

313
00:14:49,620 --> 00:14:52,763
Scientists often report a number called a p-value,

314
00:14:52,763 --> 00:14:56,521
and it's very often described as the chance that a result is a

315
00:14:56,521 --> 00:14:56,950
fluke.

316
00:14:57,300 --> 00:15:00,780
That description flips the question, just like mistake one.

317
00:15:01,130 --> 00:15:04,393
Next time, we'll see what a p-value really measures,

318
00:15:04,393 --> 00:15:06,350
and why Bayes is the missing piece.

319
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,060
I will see you in the next video.
